description:
## Context

The client ran a product analytics dashboard on a single Postgres primary. Ingest was fine; **reads were the problem**. Every chart executed three to five aggregate queries against tables with billions of rows, and p95 crossed two seconds for anyone outside Europe.

I was brought in for six weeks to make dashboards fast without migrating the data warehouse. The constraint that shaped everything: *no loss of historical accuracy*.

## What I built

- **Regional ingest workers** on Cloudflare Workers, writing pre-aggregated 10-second buckets into Durable Objects.
- **A query service** that fans out to the three closest regions, merges partial buckets, and returns a single series.
- **A backfill path** that replays object-storage archives into the rollup store, idempotent by bucket key.
- **The chart layer**: a small React component with virtualised points and a Web Worker for downsampling.

```ts
// merge partial rollups from N regions
const merged = regions
  .flatMap((r) => r.buckets)
  .reduce((acc, b) => {
    const cur = acc.get(b.ts) ?? { ts: b.ts, sum: 0, count: 0 };
    cur.sum += b.sum;
    cur.count += b.count;
    return acc.set(b.ts, cur);
  }, new Map<number, Bucket>());
```

## Outcome

p95 dashboard query latency dropped from **1,940 ms to 42 ms** globally. Ingest cost fell 31% because rollups are written once instead of being recomputed per query. The team kept their warehouse and their SQL skills — the migration surface was three endpoints.

## What I would do differently

The first version keyed buckets by wall-clock second, which made late events land in the wrong bucket during clock skew. We moved to event-time bucketing with a five-second allowed lateness window in week three. That should have been the design from day one.
